// 271 — throw / raise an exception — DESIGN NOTE
// Exceptions (try/throw/catch in C++/Java/Python) implement non-local
// control flow: a function deep in the call stack signals failure,
// and the runtime unwinds frames until something catches. Convenient
// to write; expensive to read — at any call site in a "throwing"
// function, you have to assume an exception path could leave through
// it, and your cleanup story has to handle that case.
//
// AiLang has no exception machinery. The two patterns that cover
// the same use cases:
//
//   1. **Sentinel return values.** Functions that can fail return a
//      negative Integer. The caller checks; control flow stays
//      lexical. See 286_validate_inputs.ailang.
//
//   2. **HaltProgram(code) for unrecoverable invariant violations.**
//      Equivalent to "uncaught exception" — the program ends and
//      the OS sees the exit code. See 285_assertions.ailang.
//
// Both have the same ergonomic property: every call's failure mode
// is visible at the call site. There is no hidden alternate path.
SubRoutine.Main {
    PrintMessage("AiLang failure idiom: negative-Integer sentinel returns\n")
    PrintMessage("  see 285 (assert / halt) and 286 (validate inputs)\n")
    PrintMessage("AiLang has no try/throw/catch; failure is data, not control flow\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
